(a) (a) Requirement for a food safety plan.
(a)(1) (1) You must prepare, or have prepared, and implement a written food safety plan.
(a)(2) (2) The food safety plan must be prepared, or its preparation overseen, by one or more preventive controls qualified individuals.
(b) (b) Contents of a food safety plan. The written food safety plan must include:
(b)(1) (1) The written hazard analysis as required by § 117.130(a)(2);
(b)(2) (2) The written preventive controls as required by § 117.135(b);
(b)(3) (3) The written supply-chain program as required by subpart G of this part;
(b)(4) (4) The written recall plan as required by § 117.139(a); and
(b)(5) (5) The written procedures for monitoring the implementation of the preventive controls as required by § 117.145(a);
(b)(6) (6) The written corrective action procedures as required by § 117.150(a)(1); and
(b)(7) (7) The written verification procedures as required by § 117.165(b).
(c) (c) Records. The food safety plan required by this section is a record that is subject to the requirements of subpart F of this part.

(a) (a) Requirement for a hazard analysis.
(a)(1) (1) You must conduct a hazard analysis to identify and evaluate, based on experience, illness data, scientific reports, and other information, known or reasonably foreseeable hazards for each type of food manufactured, processed, packed, or held at your facility to determine whether there are any hazards requiring a preventive control.
(a)(2) (2) The hazard analysis must be written regardless of its outcome.
(b) (b) Hazard identification. The hazard identification must consider:
(b)(1) (1) Known or reasonably foreseeable hazards that include:
(b)(1)(i) (i) Biological hazards, including microbiological hazards such as parasites, environmental pathogens, and other pathogens;
(b)(1)(ii) (ii) Chemical hazards, including radiological hazards, substances such as pesticide and drug residues, natural toxins, decomposition, unapproved food or color additives, and food allergens; and
(b)(1)(iii) (iii) Physical hazards (such as stones, glass, and metal fragments); and
(b)(2) (2) Known or reasonably foreseeable hazards that may be present in the food for any of the following reasons:
(b)(2)(i) (i) The hazard occurs naturally;
(b)(2)(ii) (ii) The hazard may be unintentionally introduced; or
(b)(2)(iii) (iii) The hazard may be intentionally introduced for purposes of economic gain.
(c) (c) Hazard evaluation.
(c)(1)(i) (1)(i) The hazard analysis must include an evaluation of the hazards identified in paragraph (b) of this section to assess the severity of the illness or injury if the hazard were to occur and the probability that the hazard will occur in the absence of preventive controls.
(c)(1)(ii) (ii) The hazard evaluation required by paragraph (c)(1)(i) of this section must include an evaluation of environmental pathogens whenever a ready-to-eat food is exposed to the environment prior to packaging and the packaged food does not receive a treatment or otherwise include a control measure (such as a formulation lethal to the pathogen) that would significantly minimize the pathogen.
(c)(2) (2) The hazard evaluation must consider the effect of the following on the safety of the finished food for the intended consumer:
(c)(2)(i) (i) The formulation of the food;
(c)(2)(ii) (ii) The condition, function, and design of the facility and equipment;
(c)(2)(iii) (iii) Raw materials and other ingredients;
(c)(2)(iv) (iv) Transportation practices;
(c)(2)(v) (v) Manufacturing/processing procedures;
(c)(2)(vi) (vi) Packaging activities and labeling activities;
(c)(2)(vii) (vii) Storage and distribution;
(c)(2)(viii) (viii) Intended or reasonably foreseeable use;
(c)(2)(ix) (ix) Sanitation, including employee hygiene; and
(c)(2)(x) (x) Any other relevant factors, such as the temporal (e.g., weather-related) nature of some hazards (e.g., levels of some natural toxins).

(a) (a) You must validate that the preventive controls identified and implemented in accordance with § 117.135 are adequate to control the hazard as appropriate to the nature of the preventive control and its role in the facility's food safety system.
(b) (b) The validation of the preventive controls:
(b)(1) (1) Must be performed (or overseen) by a preventive controls qualified individual:
(b)(1)(i)(A) (i)(A) Prior to implementation of the food safety plan; or
(b)(1)(i)(B) (B) When necessary to demonstrate the control measures can be implemented as designed:
(b)(1)(i)(B)(1) (1) Within 90 calendar days after production of the applicable food first begins; or
(b)(1)(i)(B)(2) (2) Within a reasonable timeframe, provided that the preventive controls qualified individual prepares (or oversees the preparation of) a written justification for a timeframe that exceeds 90 calendar days after production of the applicable food first begins;
(b)(1)(ii) (ii) Whenever a change to a control measure or combination of control measures could impact whether the control measure or combination of control measures, when properly implemented, will effectively control the hazards; and
(b)(1)(iii) (iii) Whenever a reanalysis of the food safety plan reveals the need to do so;
(b)(2) (2) Must include obtaining and evaluating scientific and technical evidence (or, when such evidence is not available or is inadequate, conducting studies) to determine whether the preventive controls, when properly implemented, will effectively control the hazards; and
(c) (c) You do not need to validate:
(c)(1) (1) The food allergen controls in § 117.135(c)(2);
(c)(2) (2) The sanitation controls in § 117.135(c)(3);
(c)(3) (3) The recall plan in § 117.139;
(c)(4) (4) The supply-chain program in subpart G of this part; and
(c)(5) (5) Other preventive controls, if the preventive controls qualified individual prepares (or oversees the preparation of) a written justification that validation is not applicable based on factors such as the nature of the hazard, and the nature of the preventive control and its role in the facility's food safety system.

(a) (a) One or more preventive controls qualified individuals must do or oversee the following:
(a)(1) (1) Preparation of the food safety plan (§ 117.126(a)(2));
(a)(2) (2) Validation of the preventive controls (§ 117.160(b)(1));
(a)(3) (3) Written justification for validation to be performed in a timeframe that exceeds the first 90 calendar days of production of the applicable food;
(a)(4) (4) Determination that validation is not required (§ 117.160(c)(5));
(a)(5) (5) Review of records (§ 117.165(a)(4));
(a)(6) (6) Written justification for review of records of monitoring and corrective actions within a timeframe that exceeds 7 working days;
(a)(7) (7) Reanalysis of the food safety plan (§ 117.170(d)); and
(a)(8) (8) Determination that reanalysis can be completed, and additional preventive controls validated, as appropriate to the nature of the preventive control and its role in the facility's food safety system, in a timeframe that exceeds the first 90 calendar days of production of the applicable food.
(b) (b) A qualified auditor must conduct an onsite audit (§ 117.435(a)).
(c)(1) (c)(1) To be a preventive controls qualified individual, the individual must have successfully completed training in the development and application of risk-based preventive controls at least equivalent to that received under a standardized curriculum recognized as adequate by FDA or be otherwise qualified through job experience to develop and apply a food safety system. Job experience may qualify an individual to perform these functions if such experience has provided an individual with knowledge at least equivalent to that provided through the standardized curriculum. This individual may be, but is not required to be, an employee of the facility.
(c)(2) (2) To be a qualified auditor, a qualified individual must have technical expertise obtained through education, training, or experience (or a combination thereof) necessary to perform the auditing function.
(d) (d) All applicable training in the development and application of risk-based preventive controls must be documented in records, including the date of the training, the type of training, and the person(s) trained.

(a) (a) If a facility that is solely engaged in the storage of unexposed packaged food stores any such refrigerated packaged food that requires time/temperature control to significantly minimize or prevent the growth of, or toxin production by pathogens, the facility must conduct the following activities as appropriate to ensure the effectiveness of the temperature controls:
(a)(1) (1) Establish and implement temperature controls adequate to significantly minimize or prevent the growth of, or toxin production by, pathogens;
(a)(2) (2) Monitor the temperature controls with adequate frequency to provide assurance that the temperature controls are consistently performed;
(a)(3) (3) If there is a loss of temperature control that may impact the safety of such refrigerated packaged food, take appropriate corrective actions to:
(a)(3)(i) (i) Correct the problem and reduce the likelihood that the problem will recur;
(a)(3)(ii) (ii) Evaluate all affected food for safety; and
(a)(3)(iii) (iii) Prevent the food from entering commerce, if you cannot ensure the affected food is not adulterated under section 402 of the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act;
(a)(4) (4) Verify that temperature controls are consistently implemented by:
(a)(4)(i) (i) Calibrating temperature monitoring and recording devices (or checking them for accuracy);
(a)(4)(ii) (ii) Reviewing records of calibration within a reasonable time after the records are created; and
(a)(4)(iii) (iii) Reviewing records of monitoring and corrective actions taken to correct a problem with the control of temperature within 7 working days after the records are created or within a reasonable timeframe, provided that the preventive controls qualified individual prepares (or oversees the preparation of) a written justification for a timeframe that exceeds 7 working days;
(a)(5) (5) Establish and maintain the following records:
(a)(5)(i) (i) Records (whether affirmative records demonstrating temperature is controlled or exception records demonstrating loss of temperature control) documenting the monitoring of temperature controls for any such refrigerated packaged food;
(a)(5)(ii) (ii) Records of corrective actions taken when there is a loss of temperature control that may impact the safety of any such refrigerated packaged food; and
(a)(5)(iii) (iii) Records documenting verification activities.
(b) (b) The records that a facility must establish and maintain under paragraph (a)(5) of this section are subject to the requirements of subpart F of this part.

(a) (a) Be kept as original records, true copies (such as photocopies, pictures, scanned copies, microfilm, microfiche, or other accurate reproductions of the original records), or electronic records;
(b) (b) Contain the actual values and observations obtained during monitoring and, as appropriate, during verification activities;
(c) (c) Be accurate, indelible, and legible;
(d) (d) Be created concurrently with performance of the activity documented;
(e) (e) Be as detailed as necessary to provide history of work performed; and
(f) (f) Include:
(f)(1) (1) Information adequate to identify the plant or facility (e.g., the name, and when necessary, the location of the plant or facility);
(f)(2) (2) The date and, when appropriate, the time of the activity documented;
(f)(3) (3) The signature or initials of the person performing the activity; and
(f)(4) (4) Where appropriate, the identity of the product and the lot code, if any.
(g) (g) Records that are established or maintained to satisfy the requirements of this part and that meet the definition of electronic records in § 11.3(b)(6) of this chapter are exempt from the requirements of part 11 of this chapter. Records that satisfy the requirements of this part, but that also are required under other applicable statutory provisions or regulations, remain subject to part 11 of this chapter.
(section) Records must:

(a) (a) The supply-chain program must include:
(a)(1) (1) Using approved suppliers as required by § 117.420;
(a)(2) (2) Determining appropriate supplier verification activities (including determining the frequency of conducting the activity) as required by § 117.425;
(a)(3) (3) Conducting supplier verification activities as required by §§ 117.430 and 117.435;
(a)(4) (4) Documenting supplier verification activities as required by § 117.475; and
(a)(5) (5) When applicable, verifying a supply-chain-applied control applied by an entity other than the receiving facility's supplier and documenting that verification as required by § 117.475, or obtaining documentation of an appropriate verification activity from another entity, reviewing and assessing that documentation, and documenting the review and assessment as required by § 117.475.
(b) (b) The following are appropriate supplier verification activities for raw materials and other ingredients:
(b)(1) (1) Onsite audits;
(b)(2) (2) Sampling and testing of the raw material or other ingredient;
(b)(3) (3) Review of the supplier's relevant food safety records; and
(b)(4) (4) Other appropriate supplier verification activities based on supplier performance and the risk associated with the raw material or other ingredient.
(c) (c) The supply-chain program must provide assurance that a hazard requiring a supply-chain-applied control has been significantly minimized or prevented.
(d)(1) (d)(1) Except as provided by paragraph (d)(2) of this section, in approving suppliers and determining the appropriate supplier verification activities and the frequency with which they are conducted, the following must be considered:
(d)(1)(i) (i) The hazard analysis of the food, including the nature of the hazard controlled before receipt of the raw material or other ingredient, applicable to the raw material and other ingredients;
(d)(1)(ii) (ii) The entity or entities that will be applying controls for the hazards requiring a supply-chain-applied control;
(d)(1)(iii) (iii) Supplier performance, including:
(d)(1)(iii)(A) (A) The supplier's procedures, processes, and practices related to the safety of the raw material and other ingredients;
(d)(1)(iii)(B) (B) Applicable FDA food safety regulations and information relevant to the supplier's compliance with those regulations, including an FDA warning letter or import alert relating to the safety of food and other FDA compliance actions related to food safety (or, when applicable, relevant laws and regulations of a country whose food safety system FDA has officially recognized as comparable or has determined to be equivalent to that of the United States, and information relevant to the supplier's compliance with those laws and regulations); and
(d)(1)(iii)(C) (C) The supplier's food safety history relevant to the raw materials or other ingredients that the receiving facility receives from the supplier, including available information about results from testing raw materials or other ingredients for hazards, audit results relating to the safety of the food, and responsiveness of the supplier in correcting problems; and
(d)(1)(iv) (iv) Any other factors as appropriate and necessary, such as storage and transportation practices.
(d)(2) (2) Considering supplier performance can be limited to the supplier's compliance history as required by paragraph (d)(1)(iii)(B) of this section, if the supplier is:
(d)(2)(i) (i) A qualified facility as defined by § 117.3;
